... you have to work around IE problems when you develop cross-browser script
... you don't get anything automatically and people describe what they are doing.
... aria does not drive the behavior
notes comments such as &quot; the people who are implementing svg tiny would be a problem.&quot;
jon gunderson of the university of illinois is working on some IE ARIA demos, as is charles chen, dev of FireVox
notes that its too bad &quot;the people&quot; weren't informed of the meeting being held in their names...
Ok. This, to me, is a problem in itself.
me too...
I am certainly not going to back any sort of decision made in a meeting of that kind.
so are remarks such as: &lt;hsivonen&gt; (I don't really believe in Web language versioning
i'll make an inquiry on the PF list about the meeting and innocently raise the issue of not pre-publicizing the meeting so that there could be broader representation
tina: do you have member access?
50fa2422c663a31e707bdd4f9e4ed4c5: to W3C internals? Yes.
the only thing i could find in the PF archives is an after-the-fact post: http://lists.w3.org/Archives/Member/w3c-wai-pf/2007OctDec/0052.html
i'm going to reply to the reply from the chair AlG on list
there was no one in the XHTML2 WG that had been informed (save, apparently, for StevenP, but he's unavailable for meetings this week, right?)
From a methodology point of view, this is unfortunate. The agreements made during that meeting cannot be considered valid for the XHTML WG. From what I could see, Steven was supposed to be there. He was ALSO supposed to chair a meeting here today.
